In article <35870@shemp.CS.UCLA.EDU> claus@maui.cs.ucla.edu (Claus Giloi) writes:
>
>C'mon Richard. The kind souls of the dev team are probably using
>time they can't afford to work on nethack. They don't OWE you or
>me anything.
>
I know some of them personally to be kind souls.  It is common knowledge
that they have enough time to send email.  I think a response on the level of

    (a) that's a nice idea, we'll code it up
    (b) that's a nice idea, but too much work, we'll see if someone volunteers
    (c) that's a terrible idea, forget it

is called for.  Rather than a "debt," that is "politeness."

As for debts, I think that they do *owe* us a list of known serious
bugs, posted here on a regular basis.  Instead I get complaints from
them about how people keep emailing to them about "known" bugs.  When
I used to maintain a game program, I felt sorry for people who got
wiped out because of *my* programming mistakes.  Eventually I made
sure that the game was recoverable after crashes.  Apparently such a
feature has been implemented in nethack by a reader of this newsgroup;
if it works, I think that the dev team "owes" it to us to distribute
that in pl8.

>Besides, I remember when there was no 'confirm' for shopkeepers.
>Maybe not realistic ( Perusing, you stab Assidhopo --more-- ), but
>fun because it made you _careful_.

There is still no confirmation explicitly for shopkeepers (why should
they be any different from eyes and nurses?).  The confirmation is
requested because they are peaceful, so you need not be deprived of
your *fun*.  Just set the option !confirm and have more fun than
a barrel of monkeys.

In a different note, Kent complained that people who have not won
should not be making suggestions that would make the game easier to
play.  For the record, I have won, as have several other people who
post regularly here.  I'm for democracy on this issue: all players'
opinions are equally important.  (Even Kent's :-))

